With die 1 revealed as 5, the fair value is 5 + 7 = 12. The last counterparty sold to me at 10. If they were informed, that would mean die 2 + die 3 is below 5, so I nudge my estimate down to about 11.5.

I'm long 1, so I shift the quote slightly lower to lean toward selling. I keep the spread wide because the remaining two dice still carry a lot of uncertainty.

{"bid": 9.5, "ask": 13.1}

Die 1 = 6, so the expected sum is 6 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 13. Trading history in stage 2 is empty, so the public fair value is about 13.

The remaining two dice give a settlement range of 8 to 18, with a standard deviation of about 2.4. An informed trader trades whenever its value is outside my quotes, so I quote a spread of about ±1.5 around 13.

{"bid": 11.5, "ask": 14.5}

Dice 1 and 2 total 8, and die 3 is uniform on 1 to 6. So settlement is between 9 and 14, with a fair value of 11.5. The two trades so far in stage 3 (one buy, one sell) point in opposite directions and roughly cancel.

I'm quoting about 1.6 either side of fair. An informed trader would only trade with me on the extreme outcomes (14 or 9), and the loss there is small. Noise traders cross each side about 30% of the time, earning 1.6 per trade.

{"bid": 9.9, "ask": 13.1}

Expected values: die1 = (5+5+6+7+8+11)/6 = 42/6 = 7. Die2 = 0.5·2 + 0.2·6 + 0.3·8 = 1 + 1.2 + 2.4 = 4.6. Die3 = 32/4 = 8. Prior mean = 19.6.

The fill at 21.8 hints the true value may be higher, so I'll shade my estimate up slightly to about 20.2. Since I'm short, I'll skew my quotes up as well.

{"bid": 18.2, "ask": 22.6}

Expected sum is 6.75 + 6.8 + 4.875 = 18.425, with a standard deviation of about 6.5.

Last quote had no trade, which hints the true value lies inside the old range. That is only weak evidence, so I keep the fair value near 18.4. Uncertainty is still high, so I keep a wide, roughly symmetric spread around it.

{"bid": 13.6, "ask": 23.3}

Fair value is about 19.8. The expected values of the three dice are 8, 5.33 and 6.5. The total has a standard deviation of about 5.7.

Informed traders arrive 35% of the time and know the settlement value exactly. To protect against them, I quote roughly 4 points either side of fair value. That width still leaves room to trade with noise traders, whose valuations spread about 5.7 around fair value.

{"bid": 15.8, "ask": 23.9}

Fair value is about 18.2. Die 3 shows 7, and the expected values of die 1 (5.75) and die 2 (5) add up to 17.75. I nudge that up slightly because someone bought from me at 19, which hints the true value may be higher.

The two unrevealed dice leave a standard deviation of about 5, so I quote a fairly wide spread around 18.2. I'm short one unit, so I shift both prices up a little to make another buy from me less likely.

{"bid": 15.8, "ask": 20.6}

Fair value is about 17.75. That is die 1's mean of 5.75, plus die 2's mean of 5, plus the revealed die 3 of 7.

Someone sold to me at 15.6. That could be an informed trader, so I shade fair value slightly lower, to about 17.4. I'll quote roughly ±2.3 around that.

{"bid": 15.2, "ask": 19.8}

Expected value is about 25.6: the 17 cards sum to 145, so the average card is about 8.53, and three cards give about 25.6. The standard deviation of the three-card sum is about 5.5.

About 35% of counterparties know the settlement value exactly, so trades against them lose money. I quote roughly ±4 around fair value to cover that.

{"bid": 21.6, "ask": 29.6}

Card 1 is a Queen (12). The other 51 cards sum to 352, so each remaining card averages about 6.90. With two cards still hidden, fair value is about 12 + 13.8 = **25.8**.

The two hidden cards add a spread of about ±5.2 (one standard deviation) around that. So I quote roughly ±3 around fair to cover the risk of trading against informed counterparties. I'm already long 1, so I shift both prices down a little to make selling more likely than buying.

{"bid": 22.6, "ask": 28.6}

With card 1 = 12 and card 2 = 4 revealed, the 50 cards left sum to 364 − 16 = 348. That averages 6.96 per card, so fair value is 16 + 6.96 ≈ 22.96.

Card 3 can be anything from 1 to 13, so its value is spread widely (sd ≈ 3.7). An informed counterparty knows the exact total, which can fall anywhere from 17 to 29. That calls for a wide spread, roughly 4 points either side of fair value. I'm long 1, so I shade both prices slightly down to make selling a bit more likely than buying.

{"bid": 18.6, "ask": 27.0}

The one trade so far tells us something. Before any trade, three cards average about 21 with a standard deviation of roughly 6.3. Someone sold to me at 13, which only happens if they valued the contract below 13. An informed trader does that when the true sum is under 13, which has probability about 0.09. A noise trader does it with probability about 0.11. Given the sale, it is about equally likely to have come from either type. If informed, the sum is probably around 10; if noise, it tells us nothing and the mean stays 21. That puts fair value at roughly 15.5 to 16.

I'll quote a wide spread around about 16, since uncertainty is still high and informed flow is heavy. I'm leaning slightly toward selling to lighten my long position.

{"bid": 11, "ask": 21}

Fair value is 21. Each card averages 7, so three cards average 21. The spread of the sum is wide, with a standard deviation of about 6.4.

Last quote's no-trade tells us little. I'll keep a wide spread, a bit tighter than before, to protect against informed traders.

{"bid": 17, "ask": 25}

Cards 1 and 2 are known: 8 + 12 = 20. That leaves 50 cards in the deck, with a total value of 364 − 20 = 344. So card 3 is worth 344 / 50 ≈ 6.88 on average, and the fair value is about 26.9.

I am short 3 and can sell only one more unit, so I keep a fairly wide spread to protect against informed traders. I also lean the quote up a little to favour buying back.

{"bid": 24.5, "ask": 29.5}

The contract's expected value is 21, since each card averages 7 and three cards are dealt. The sum has a standard deviation of about 6.4.

Informed traders know the actual sum and will only trade when it beats my price. Noise traders' valuations are spread about 6.4 around 21. A spread of roughly ±4.5 around 21 should earn enough from noise traders to cover losses to informed ones.

{"bid": 16.5, "ask": 25.5}

The expected value of one card is (4·2 + 3·10 + 4·13 + 2·14)/13 = 118/13 ≈ 9.08, so the three-card sum has a fair value of about 27.2. Its standard deviation is about 7.8, after adjusting for dealing without replacement.

Informed traders make up half the flow, so I quote fairly wide around fair value to limit losses to them.

{"bid": 22.5, "ask": 32.0}

The deck's values sum to 78, so the average card is 7.8 and the expected value of a 3-card sum is 23.4.

The first quote (19 / 27.8) got no trade. That hints slightly that the value lies inside that range, but it doesn't move the centre much. I'll keep roughly the same width around 23.4, which leaves room for informed traders on either side.

{"bid": 19.5, "ask": 27.5}

Fair value is 12.25, the expected product of two independent dice (3.5 × 3.5).

I'm quoting about 6 either side of fair value. That gives some protection against informed traders, since the product's standard deviation is about 9. It is still tight enough to trade often with noise traders, whose valuations vary by 13.

{"bid": 6.2, "ask": 18.3}

Fair value is about 12.2. The 13-card deck sums to 53, so each card averages about 4.08, and three cards average 12.23. The settlement standard deviation is about 6.

I'm quoting about 3.8 either side of fair value. That spread should cover losses to informed traders while still trading with most noise traders.

{"bid": 8.4, "ask": 16.0}

The fair value before anything is revealed is 3.5 × 3.5 = 12.25. The product of the two dice is spread out widely, from 1 to 36. Noise traders' valuations scatter around 12.25 with a standard deviation of 13.

I am quoting about ±4.75 around fair value. That is wide enough to limit losses to informed traders, who see the true product. It is still tight enough to catch a good share of the noise flow.

{"bid": 7.5, "ask": 17.0}

The expected value of the product is 3.5 × 3.5 = 12.25. The last quote (7.5 bid, 17 ask) drew no trade, which hints the true value sits inside that range, so 12.25 is still the right centre.

The settlement value is widely spread (standard deviation about 8.9), so an informed trader could cost a lot on either side. I'll quote about 3.75 either side of fair value, a bit tighter than last time, to trade more with noise traders.

{"bid": 8.5, "ask": 16}

The fair value is the expected product of two independent dice: 3.5 × 3.5 = 12.25. The product is widely spread, with a standard deviation of about 8.9.

I quote a fairly wide spread around 12.25 so that informed traders only hit me when the value is far from fair. Noise traders, with a standard deviation of 13, should still trade with me often enough to pay for that risk.

{"bid": 6.0, "ask": 18.5}

Card 2 is the only 1 in the deck, so the minimum, and the settlement value, is certainly 1. An informed counterparty values the contract at 1 and will never buy above that.

A noise trader values it at 1 plus Normal noise with standard deviation 2.8. If I set the ask at 1 + x, my expected profit is 0.5 × x × P(noise > x). This peaks at about x ≈ 0.75 × 2.8 ≈ 2.1, so I ask 3.1.

{"bid": null, "ask": 3.1}

The second flip came up tails, so the coin must be the fair one (a P(heads)=1 coin cannot show tails). That leaves 4 flips still unknown, each heads with probability 0.5. So the settlement is 1 + Binomial(4, 0.5). It ranges from 1 to 5 and its fair value is 3.

I'm long 2, so I shade the quote slightly downward to encourage selling. Informed traders hit me only on the tail outcomes. Noise traders cross a spread of about ±0.6 most of the time, which should keep the trades profitable on average.

{"bid": 2.3, "ask": 3.5}

Current fair value: 4 heads in 7 flips gives a 21.8% chance the coin is the 0.8 one. That makes P(flip 8 = heads) ≈ 0.566, so the fair value is about 20 + 5 × 0.566 ≈ 22.8. The contract will settle at either 20 or 25.

Quote choice: a bid at 20 and an ask at 25 mean an informed trader never trades with me profitably, because trades only happen strictly through my prices. Noise traders still trade with me at an edge of about 2.2 to 2.8 versus fair value.

{"bid": 20.0, "ask": 25.0}

Prior fair value: E[p] = 0.4·0.5 + 0.6·0.7 = 0.62, so the expected settlement is 70 × 0.62 = 43.4.

The settlement value is quite uncertain, with a standard deviation of about 14. About 35% of counterparties are informed, so I quote a fairly wide spread of about ±7 around fair value.

{"bid": 36.4, "ask": 50.4}

Posterior after 4 heads: prior 0.4*0.0625=0.025 vs 0.6*0.2401=0.14406. P(0.7)=0.14406/0.16906≈0.852. Expected p≈0.852*0.7+0.148*0.5=0.670. Remaining 3 flips: 2.01 heads. Fair ≈ 40+20.1=60.1.

Trading history adds a little info; ignore mostly. Quote around 60 with spread.

{"bid": 55, "ask": 65.5}

After four heads in four flips, the coin is very likely the 0.7 coin. Its posterior probability is about 0.85, so the expected heads rate is about 0.67. The fair value is 40 + 3 × 10 × 0.67 ≈ 60.1. The settlement value can only be 40, 50, 60 or 70.

I'm quoting a wide spread around fair value to protect against informed traders. I've shaded it slightly upward because I'm short one unit.

{"bid": 55, "ask": 65.5}

Before any flips are revealed, the expected chance of heads is 0.6 × 0.4 + 0.4 × 0.8 = 0.56. Over 6 flips that gives a fair value of 6 × 0.56 = 3.36.

The settlement value could be anywhere from 0 to 6. Informed traders will pick me off whenever the true count is outside my quotes. Noise traders' valuations are spread with a standard deviation of 1.6. So I am quoting about 1 point either side of fair value, which still trades with many noise traders while limiting losses to informed ones.

{"bid": 2.3, "ask": 4.4}

Fair value is about 15.5. With 2 heads in 4 flips, the posterior puts about 0.77 on the fair coin and 0.23 on the 0.7 coin. That gives P(heads) ≈ 0.546, so the last 2 flips should add about 1.09 heads.

The settlement can only be 10, 15 or 20. A bid of 9.9 and an ask of 20.1 sit just outside those values. An informed trader therefore never trades against me, and noise traders still give positive edge.

{"bid": 9.9, "ask": 20.1}

Updated fair value is about 7.2. The buy at 8 was strong evidence that the value is 9 or 10. An informed trader buys at 8 only if the value is 9 or 10. A noise trader buys only about 11% of the time. Weighting the posterior that way gives an expected value of about 7.2.

I'm quoting wide around that because informed flow is likely, and I'm skewing slightly toward buying back since I'm already short.

{"bid": 5.6, "ask": 9.0}

Given one red and two blue in the first three draws, and a uniform prior on the number of red balls, the expected settlement is about 38. That comes from 1 red already drawn, plus 7 remaining balls × 0.4 expected red fraction ≈ 2.8, for 3.8 reds × 10.

The recent buys at my asks (49, 38, 33) hint at upside, so I treat fair value as roughly 38–40. I'm short 3, so buying one back is useful. A bid a little under fair value should still get filled often by noise traders while keeping some edge.

{"bid": 31, "ask": null}

After draw 1 came up red, the posterior on the number of red balls R is proportional to R. That makes the expected value E[R] = Σ R² / Σ R = 385/55 = 7.

Earlier stage-1 trades hint the value may sit slightly higher. I'm also short 1, so I shade both quotes a little upward around 7.

{"bid": 6.2, "ask": 8.2}

With draw 1 red, the posterior on red balls is proportional to R. That gives an expected value of 385/55 = 7.

The last trade was a sale to me at 6.2, which hints the value may be a bit lower. I put fair value near 6.8 and quote a wide spread around it.

{"bid": 5.9, "ask": 7.8}

With two red balls drawn out of two, the urn very likely holds many red balls. Each possible red count R was equally likely at the start. The chance of drawing two reds in a row is proportional to R(R−1), so the expected red count is now Σ R²(R−1) / Σ R(R−1) = 2640 / 330 = 8.0.

The last counterparty bought at 8.3, which hints the true value may be slightly above 8, but only weakly. Noise traders' valuations are spread widely (standard deviation 4.7), so a wide spread is still fine. I'm long 2 units, so I'll set my quotes slightly lower to make selling more likely than buying.

{"bid": 6.6, "ask": 9.0}

After one red draw, the posterior on the number of red balls R is proportional to R. That gives a fair value of E[R] = 385/55 = 7, with a standard deviation of about 2.45.

I'm quoting wide around 7 because informed traders are a large share of the flow (35%). An informed buyer only lifts my ask if R = 10, which has probability 10/55. An informed seller only hits my bid if R ≤ 4, which has probability 10/55. Noise traders, whose valuations spread around 7 with standard deviation 2.4, still trade at these prices often enough to earn the spread.

{"bid": 4.5, "ask": 9.5}

After draw 1 came up red, the urn odds tilt toward more red balls, so the expected count started at 7. The sale to me at 4.5 and the lack of a trade at 3.3/7.3 both pull that down. My updated estimate is about 5.3.

I'm quoting roughly ±2 around 5.3. I'm already long one unit, so I shade both prices slightly lower to make selling a little more likely than buying.

{"bid": 3.4, "ask": 7.2}

With one red and one blue drawn, the expected number of red balls is 5, with a standard deviation of about 2.2. Earlier trading adds little information: one counterparty sold to me and one bought from me, which roughly cancels out.

Informed traders know the exact count, so the spread needs to stay wide to protect against them. My last quote of 3.4 / 6.6 got no trade, so I'm tightening slightly around 5 to make a trade a bit more likely.

{"bid": 3.6, "ask": 6.4}

Prior mean is 60 and the standard deviation is 10·√2.4 ≈ 15.5. No trade happened at 52/68, which is only weak information, so fair value stays at about 60.

The informed trader's adverse selection is large here. Noise traders' valuations have a standard deviation of 12, and informed traders' have about 15.5. A spread of about ±7 seems reasonable.

{"bid": 53, "ask": 67}

Given the first draw was blue, the other 9 balls are each red with probability 0.6, so the expected number of reds is about 5.4. Both earlier trades were counterparties selling to me at my bid, which hints the true count is lower. My estimate of fair value is about 5.0.

I am long 2 with a limit of 3, so I set the quote lower than my estimate. This makes a sell more likely, which reduces my position, and a further buy less likely.

{"bid": 3.7, "ask": 5.5}

Given 3 draws (2 red), the remaining 8 balls are independently red with probability 0.3, so the expected count is 2 + 2.4 = 4.4 and the expected value is 44. Sells at our bid hint slightly lower, but the informed fraction is modest.

Settlement value = 20 + 10·Bin(8, 0.3), with a standard deviation of about 13. We are long 2, so I skew the quotes slightly lower.

